\name{Ggplot2AreaLayerProcessor}
\alias{Ggplot2AreaLayerProcessor}
\title{ggplot2 Area Layer Processor}
\description{
Processes \code{geom_area()} into MAIDR's \code{area}, \code{stacked_area} and
\code{stacked_normalized_area} layers.

Before this processor existed these layers fell through to
\code{Ggplot2UnknownLayerProcessor}, so an area chart carried no data at all.
\subsection{Why not read it as a line}{

In a stacked area chart the band's \strong{top edge} is a cumulative total while
the band's \strong{height} is the series' own value. A line layer announces one
number per point with nothing to say which of those two it is, so the type
exists to keep them apart: MAIDR's area trace announces the series' value
and reports the running total beside it.
}

\subsection{The two traps in ggplot2's built data}{

\strong{\code{y} is not the value.} ggplot2 stacks by computing absolute band edges,
so \code{ymin}/\code{ymax} are the cumulative positions and \code{y} is the top edge. The
series' own value is \code{ymax - ymin}, and MAIDR sums the series itself to
reach the total. Emitting \code{y} would hand it a cumulative number to
accumulate again, announcing totals that grow with the number of series
rather than with the data.

\strong{Most of the rows are not data.} \code{geom_area()} defaults to
\code{stat = "align"}, which inserts interpolation vertices so the bands stack
cleanly and closes each polygon on the baseline. A four-point, two-series
chart produces twenty-four rows:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode">}}\preformatted{       x      y  ymin   ymax group align_padding
 1999.997  0.000 0.000  0.000     1          TRUE
 2000.000  5.000 2.000  5.000     1         FALSE
 2000.003  5.009 2.003  5.009     1         FALSE   <- not a data point
 2000.997  7.991 2.997  7.991     1         FALSE   <- not a data point
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

Read whole, a chart of four years announces twelve points per series,
including a reading of \code{5.009} at "year 2000.003" -- a value the data does
not hold at an x the chart does not have. \code{align_padding} does \strong{not}
identify them: it marks only the two baseline-closing vertices.

The rows that are data are those whose \code{x} the layer's own data carries,
which is verified to give the same answer as \code{stat = "identity"}.
}
}
